    <section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">CVL 434 · GEOTECHNICAL PROPERTIES OF SOILS · WINTER 2026</p><h1>Soil is<br><span>a system.</span></h1><p class="intro">A five-part laboratory investigation of how soil classifies, compacts, drains, settles, and resists failure. Each experiment isolates one behaviour; together, they inform the ground conditions beneath a structure.</p><div class="chips"><span>05 experiments</span><span>Group 02</span><span>TMU</span></div></div><div class="profile"><div class="footing"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><div class="layer gravel"><b>GRAVEL / SAND</b><small>High drainage</small></div><div class="layer clay"><b>SILTY CLAY</b><small>Compressible</small></div><div class="layer dense"><b>DENSE STRATUM</b><small>Strength-bearing</small></div><div class="probe"></div><em>SITE PROFILE / NOT TO SCALE</em></div></section>
    <section class="section"><p class="eyebrow">01 / PROJECT OVERVIEW</p><div class="overview"><h2>From grain to ground response.</h2><p>The experiments use different soil samples and test setups, so this page presents a coordinated laboratory series rather than one continuous specimen history. The results form a practical toolkit: identify the material, place it correctly, predict water movement and settlement, then evaluate failure resistance.</p></div><div class="rail"><a href="#classification"><b>01</b>Classification<small>Atterberg + sieve</small></a><a href="#compaction"><b>02</b>Compaction<small>Moisture-density</small></a><a href="#permeability"><b>03</b>Permeability<small>Flow through soil</small></a><a href="#consolidation"><b>04</b>Consolidation<small>Settlement response</small></a><a href="#strength"><b>05</b>Shear strength<small>DST + UCS</small></a></div></section>
    <section class="section metrics"><p class="eyebrow">02 / KEY MEASUREMENTS</p><div><article><small>LIQUID LIMIT</small><b>28.90<span>%</span></b><p>Water content at 25 blows</p></article><article><small>MAX. DRY UNIT WEIGHT</small><b>19.54<span> kN/m³</span></b><p>At 6-7% moisture</p></article><article><small>PRECONSOLIDATION STRESS</small><b>215<span> kPa</span></b><p>Overconsolidated clay</p></article><article><small>FRICTION ANGLE</small><b>37.4<span>°</span></b><p>Direct shear result</p></article></div></section>
    <section id="classification" class="section experiment"><header><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPERIMENT 01</p><h2>Material identification</h2></div><p>Atterberg limits and sieve analysis establish the consistency and particle-size characteristics used to classify soil behaviour.</p></header><div class="grid two"><article class="card atterberg"><label>CONSISTENCY STATES <b>CLAY SAMPLE</b></label><div class="states"><span>Solid</span><span>Semi-solid</span><span class="on">Plastic</span><span>Liquid</span></div><div class="limits"><i>PL 13.37%</i><i>LL 28.90%</i><b>PI = 15.53%</b></div><p>As water content increases, the sample transitions through a workable plastic range before behaving as a liquid.</p></article><article class="copy card"><p class="eyebrow">INTERPRETATION</p><h3>Moderate plasticity range.</h3><p>The plastic-limit trials were tightly grouped: 13.33%, 13.33%, and 13.46%. The flow curve estimated the liquid limit at 28.90%.</p><code>PI = LL − PL = 28.90 − 13.37 = <b>15.53</b></code></article></div><div class="grid two lower"><article class="card grains"><label>GRAIN-SIZE COMPOSITION <b>997.7 g SAMPLE</b></label><div class="pebbles"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><p><span>● Gravel 57.47%</span><span>● Sand 39.57%</span><span>● Fines 2.96%</span></p></article><div class="table"><table><caption>Sieve analysis summary</caption><thead><tr><th>Fraction</th><th>Range</th><th>Share</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gravel</td><td>&gt;4.75 mm</td><td>57.47%</td><td>Dominant</td></tr><tr><td>Sand</td><td>0.075-4.75 mm</td><td>39.57%</td><td>Significant</td></tr><tr><td>Fines</td><td>&lt;0.075 mm</td><td>2.96%</td><td>Low</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Mass loss: 0.015%, well below the 5% acceptance check. The broad grading was interpreted as a well-graded gravelly soil with sand.</p></div></div></section>
    <section id="compaction" class="section experiment dark"><header><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPERIMENT 02</p><h2>Compaction window</h2></div><p>Standard Proctor compaction identifies the water content range that produces the greatest dry unit weight for the tested soil.</p></header><div class="grid chartrow"><article class="card chart"><label>MOISTURE-DENSITY CURVE <b>STANDARD PROCTOR</b></label><svg viewBox="0 0 620 300" role="img" aria-label="Compaction curve with maximum dry unit weight at 6 to 7 percent water content"><g class="g"><path d="M62 25V252H590M62 205H590M62 157H590M62 109H590M62 61H590"/></g><g class="t"><text x="8" y="66">20.0</text><text x="8" y="114">19.5</text><text x="8" y="162">19.0</text><text x="8" y="210">18.5</text><text x="57" y="278">0</text><text x="184" y="278">4</text><text x="311" y="278">8</text><text x="438" y="278">12</text><text x="560" y="278">16</text><text x="208" y="296">Water content, w (%)</text></g><path class="curve" d="M181 153 C223 113 248 72 285 72 S357 93 394 124 S462 196 476 225"/><g class="dots"><circle cx="181" cy="153" r="6"/><circle cx="241" cy="91" r="6"/><circle cx="285" cy="91" r="7"/><circle cx="353" cy="111" r="6"/><circle cx="476" cy="225" r="6"/></g><path class="guide" d="M267 72V252"/><text class="pt" x="276" y="52">19.54 kN/m³</text><text class="pt" x="276" y="69">optimum ≈ 6-7%</text></svg></article><article class="copy card"><p class="eyebrow">FIELD TAKEAWAY</p><h3>Add enough water to lubricate, not float.</h3><p>Dry unit weight rose from 19.03 kN/m³ at 3.73% water content to 19.54 kN/m³ at about 6-7%. Beyond optimum, water occupied void space and it fell to 18.31 kN/m³.</p><dl><dt>Mould volume</dt><dd>0.000943 m³</dd><dt>Compaction effort</dt><dd>3 layers × 25 blows</dd><dt>Specific gravity</dt><dd>2.76</dd></dl></article></div><div class="table darktable"><table><caption>Measured compaction response</caption><thead><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Water content</th><th>Dry density</th><th>Dry unit weight</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>3.73%</td><td>1,939.76 kg/m³</td><td>19.03 kN/m³</td></tr><tr class="peak"><td>2</td><td>5.62%</td><td>1,992.50 kg/m³</td><td>19.54 kN/m³</td></tr><tr class="peak"><td>3</td><td>7.03%</td><td>1,992.08 kg/m³</td><td>19.54 kN/m³</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>9.21%</td><td>1,973.59 kg/m³</td><td>19.36 kN/m³</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>13.14%</td><td>1,866.39 kg/m³</td><td>18.31 kN/m³</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
    <section id="permeability" class="section experiment"><header><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPERIMENT 03</p><h2>Water pathways</h2></div><p>Constant-head and falling-head tests quantified hydraulic conductivity for coarse and finer granular material.</p></header><div class="grid two"><article class="flow card"><div class="flowdraw constant"><b>CONSTANT HEAD</b><i></i><i></i><i></i><em></em></div><h3>Coarse gravel</h3><strong>6.87 × 10<sup>-3</sup> <small>cm/s</small></strong><p>Average k. Lower than expected for coarse gravel; fines and reduced void space were identified as likely influences.</p></article><article class="flow card"><div class="flowdraw falling"><b>FALLING HEAD</b><i></i><i></i><em></em></div><h3>Finer sand</h3><strong>1.38 × 10<sup>-3</sup> <small>cm/s</small></strong><p>Average k. The lower value is consistent with smaller pore spaces and more restricted flow.</p></article></div><div class="note"><b>Test geometry:</b> specimen diameter 10.8 cm · specimen length 11.3 cm · area 91.56 cm² · head difference 140 cm.</div></section>
    <section id="consolidation" class="section experiment pale"><header><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPERIMENT 04</p><h2>Settlement under load</h2></div><p>An oedometer test tracked void-ratio change and the time-dependent settlement response of a fine-grained soil.</p></header><div class="grid chartrow"><article class="card oed"><label>ONE-DIMENSIONAL CONSOLIDATION <b>SILTY CLAY</b></label><div class="oedometer"><div></div><i></i><b></b><em></em></div><p>Void ratio: <b>0.95</b> <span>→</span> <b>0.63</b> as effective stress increased.</p></article><article class="copy card"><p class="eyebrow">INTERPRETATION</p><h3>Overconsolidated, moderately compressible soil.</h3><p>The preconsolidation pressure of 215.44 kPa exceeded the estimated in-situ effective stress of 92 kPa, giving an OCR of 2.34.</p><dl><dt>Compression index, C<sub>c</sub></dt><dd>0.38</dd><dt>Recompression index, C<sub>r</sub></dt><dd>0.066</dd><dt>Approx. t<sub>90</sub></dt><dd>25 min</dd></dl></article></div><div class="table"><table><caption>Consolidation parameters</caption><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Measured value</th><th>Design reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Preconsolidation stress</td><td>215.44 kPa</td><td>Past stress exceeds current stress</td></tr><tr><td>Overconsolidation ratio</td><td>2.34</td><td>Overconsolidated</td></tr><tr><td>C<sub>v</sub>, log-time</td><td>1.33 × 10<sup>-3</sup> cm²/s</td><td>At 50.08% consolidation</td></tr><tr><td>C<sub>v</sub>, root-time</td><td>4.93 × 10<sup>-4</sup> cm²/s</td><td>At 90.00% consolidation</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
    <section id="strength" class="section experiment dark"><header><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPERIMENT 05</p><h2>Resistance to failure</h2></div><p>Direct shear and unconfined compression tests measured frictional and undrained strength response.</p></header><div class="grid two"><article class="strength card"><div class="shear"><i>↓</i><b></b><b></b><em>→</em></div><p class="eyebrow">DIRECT SHEAR TEST</p><h3>Mohr-Coulomb envelope</h3><dl><dt>Cohesion, c</dt><dd>8.44 kPa</dd><dt>Friction angle, φ</dt><dd>37.4°</dd><dt>Linear fit</dt><dd>R² = 0.9993</dd></dl><p>Peak shear stress: 49.54 kPa at 2 kg, 93.27 kPa at 4 kg, and 133.13 kPa at 6 kg.</p></article><article class="strength card"><div class="ucs"><i>↓</i><b></b><em></em><b></b><i>↑</i></div><p class="eyebrow">UNCONFINED COMPRESSION</p><h3>Undrained clay strength</h3><dl><dt>q<sub>u</sub>, peak stress</dt><dd>283.73 kPa</dd><dt>s<sub>u</sub> = q<sub>u</sub>/2</dt><dd>141.87 kPa</dd></dl><p>The UCS result captures the saturated cohesive sample’s failure under axial loading with no confining pressure.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section synthesis"><p class="eyebrow">SYNTHESIS / WHAT THE TESTS SAY</p><h2>Engineering decisions become clearer when each soil behaviour has a measurement behind it.</h2><div><article><b>01</b><h3>Place and compact</h3><p>The moisture-density curve defines the best working range for controlled fill.</p></article><article><b>02</b><h3>Manage water</h3><p>Hydraulic conductivity differentiates free-draining granular layers from finer material.</p></article><article><b>03</b><h3>Allow for movement</h3><p>Consolidation parameters frame time-dependent settlement expectations.</p></article><article><b>04</b><h3>Check stability</h3><p>Shear and compressive strength guide bearing and slope assessments.</p></article></div></section>
