<section class="hydro-hero"><div class="hydro-grid"></div><div class="hero-inner"><a class="back" href="index.html#projects">← Portfolio</a><p class="eyebrow">CVL 502 · HYDRAULIC ENGINEERING · WINTER 2026</p><h1>Hydraulic<br><span>Engineering</span></h1><p class="hero-summary">A hydraulic engineering lab series connecting physical testing, pipe-network analysis, open-channel behaviour, and steady-flow flood modelling.</p></div><div class="hero-diagram"><p>ENGINEERING WORKFLOW</p><div class="flow-line"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><div class="flow-labels"><span>measure</span><span>calculate</span><span>validate</span><span>model</span></div></div></section>
<section class="project-overview"><p class="eyebrow">PROJECT OVERVIEW</p><p class="overview-copy">The work moves from controlled bench-scale measurements to digital decision-making tools. Each part tests the assumptions behind the next: conservation of energy, friction and local losses, flow control in channels, then system-level modelling using EPANET and HEC-RAS.</p><div class="part-nav"><a href="#part-1"><b>01</b> Pressurized flow</a><a href="#part-2"><b>02</b> EPANET network</a><a href="#part-3"><b>03</b> Open channels</a><a href="#part-4"><b>04</b> HEC-RAS flood model</a></div></section>
<section id="part-1" class="case-part dark-part"><div class="part-heading"><span>PART 01</span><h2>Pressurized-flow<br>fundamentals</h2><p>Labs 1-3 established how energy is distributed and dissipated in closed conduits: first through a varying-area section, then along a straight pipe, and finally through fittings.</p></div><div class="diagram-panel bernoulli-diagram"><div class="head-label h-one">H1</div><div class="head-label h-two">H2</div><div class="head-label h-three">H3</div><div class="head-label h-four">H4</div><div class="head-label h-five">H5</div><div class="pipe-shape"></div><div class="energy-line"></div><div class="water-line"></div><p>Static head falls through the contraction while velocity head rises.</p></div><div class="three-cards"><article><p class="tag">LAB 01 · BERNOULLI</p><h3>Energy distribution</h3><p>Timed volumetric flow and six manometer / probe readings were used to compare static head, velocity head, and total head across a horizontal converging-diverging duct.</p><div class="micro-result"><b>1.75 × 10<sup>-4</sup></b><span>m³/s highest measured discharge</span></div></article><article><p class="tag">LAB 02 · PIPE FRICTION</p><h3>Distributed loss</h3><p>Pressure drop and discharge data were converted to Reynolds number and Darcy friction factor across low- and high-flow regimes, making the laminar/turbulent transition visible.</p><img src="assets/friction-factor-plot.png" alt="Experimental friction-factor plot from the pipe-friction lab"></article><article><p class="tag">LAB 03 · BENDS &amp; FITTINGS</p><h3>Local loss</h3><p>Mitre, elbow, short-bend, and long-bend configurations were compared using head-loss versus dynamic-head relationships and loss coefficients.</p><div class="bend-sketch"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div></article></div><div class="insight-bar"><b>Design takeaway</b><p>Pressure is not a standalone quantity: it is one component of the total energy budget. Pipe roughness, velocity, diameter, and fitting geometry all determine how much usable head remains downstream.</p></div></section>
<section id="part-2" class="case-part epanet-part"><div class="part-heading"><span>PART 02</span><h2>Pipe-network<br>modelling in EPANET</h2><p>Lab 4 scaled the loss mechanisms measured in the laboratory to a connected water-distribution system. The model was used as a sensitivity-testing environment, not just a calculator.</p></div><div class="network-layout"><div class="network-diagram"><span class="source">RESERVOIR</span><svg viewBox="0 0 700 380" role="img" aria-label="Pipe network with nodes A through H and highlighted node F"><g class="pipes"><path d="M78 193 L190 92 L330 135 L473 78 L620 126"/><path d="M78 193 L190 292 L330 250 L470 308 L620 250"/><path d="M190 92 L190 292 M330 135 L330 250 M473 78 L470 308 M620 126 L620 250 M330 135 L470 308 M330 250 L473 78"/></g><g class="nodes"><circle cx="78" cy="193" r="13"/><circle cx="190" cy="92" r="13"/><circle cx="190" cy="292" r="13"/><circle cx="330" cy="135" r="13"/><circle cx="330" cy="250" r="13"/><circle cx="473" cy="78" r="13"/><circle cx="470" cy="308" r="13"/><circle class="focus-node" cx="620" cy="126" r="17"/><circle cx="620" cy="250" r="13"/></g><g class="letters"><text x="64" y="225">A</text><text x="176" y="72">B</text><text x="176" y="327">C</text><text x="316" y="116">D</text><text x="316" y="282">E</text><text x="458" y="58">G</text><text x="455" y="342">H</text><text x="613" y="108">F</text><text x="610" y="281">J</text></g></svg><div class="network-key"><i></i> baseline pipe <i class="focus"></i> node F assessed in scenarios</div></div><div class="network-copy"><p class="eyebrow">MODEL INPUTS</p><ul><li>Junction and reservoir elevations</li><li>Pipe length, diameter, and roughness</li><li>Node demands and boundary conditions</li><li>Consistent hydraulic units</li></ul><p>Each scenario isolates one change, runs the hydraulic solver, and compares the new flow / pressure condition with the baseline.</p></div></div><div id="results" class="scenario-grid"><article><p class="tag">SCENARIO 01 · ROUGHNESS</p><h3>Lower friction in EF</h3><div class="delta"><span>Flow</span><b>80.39 → 85.00 L/s</b><span>Pressure at F</span><b>28.87 → 30.73</b></div><p>Reducing the assumed friction factor from 0.021 to 0.014 increased flow and available pressure.</p></article><article><p class="tag">SCENARIO 02 · DIAMETER</p><h3>Double pipe HF diameter</h3><div class="delta"><span>Flow in HF</span><b>86.62 → 87.87 L/s</b><span>Pressure at F</span><b>35.12 → 35.55 m</b></div><p>Head loss and velocity fell strongly; the network redistributed flow across parallel routes.</p></article><article><p class="tag">SCENARIO 03 · DEMAND</p><h3>Add 50 L/s at F</h3><div class="delta"><span>Pressure head at F</span><b>16.27 → -1.67 m</b><span>Interpretation</span><b>Service shortfall</b></div><p>A higher local demand drove a severe pressure-head reduction - a useful serviceability warning.</p></article><article><p class="tag">SCENARIO 04 · REDUNDANCY</p><h3>Add a new supply path</h3><div class="delta"><span>Pressure head at F</span><b>8.33 → 16.20 m</b><span>Flow in EF</span><b>115.38 → 114.46 L/s</b></div><p>The extra connection reduced system resistance, substantially improving pressure at F.</p></article></div></section>
<section id="part-3" class="case-part open-part"><div class="part-heading"><span>PART 03</span><h2>Controlling open-channel flow</h2><p>Labs 5 and 6 explored discharge measurement and flow regime changes in an open channel. Together, they connect geometry, depth, discharge, and energy.</p></div><div class="channel-grid"><article class="weir-card"><div class="weir-sketch"><div class="water-surface"></div><div class="notch"></div><div class="falling-water"></div></div><p class="tag">LAB 05 · WEIRS</p><h3>Calibrating discharge</h3><p>Rectangular and V-notch weirs were tested over multiple heads. Measured discharge was compared with weir equations to establish experimental coefficients.</p><div class="two-metrics"><div><b>0.257</b><span>average C<sub>d</sub>, rectangular</span></div><div><b>0.504</b><span>average C<sub>d</sub>, V-notch</span></div></div></article><article class="sluice-card"><img src="assets/sluice-specific-energy.png" alt="Specific-energy curve from the sluice-gate lab"><p class="tag">LAB 06 · SLUICE GATE</p><h3>Specific energy and alternate depths</h3><p>Ten gate positions defined a specific-energy curve, separating subcritical and supercritical behaviour while identifying the critical condition.</p><div class="result-compare"><span>Critical depth</span><b>4.21 cm theory</b><b>4.40 cm observed</b><small>4.317% difference</small></div></article></div><div class="energy-equation"><span>E = y +</span><strong>q<sup>2</sup><i>2gy<sup>2</sup></i></strong><p>At a fixed unit discharge, the minimum of the curve marks critical depth; depths on either side can carry the same specific energy.</p></div></section>
<section id="part-4" class="case-part hec-part"><div class="part-heading"><span>PART 04</span><h2>Flood-profile modelling<br>in HEC-RAS</h2><p>Lab 7 moved from physical apparatus to a steady-flow river model. Cross-section geometry, reach lengths, roughness zones, and flood-event flows were interpreted through HEC-RAS outputs.</p></div><div class="hec-layout"><div class="hec-copy"><p class="eyebrow">FALL RIVER · STATION 10</p><h3>Water-surface response by event</h3><div class="event-bars"><div><span>10-year</span><b style="width:58%">76.44 ft</b><em>Fr 0.25</em></div><div><span>50-year</span><b style="width:76%">81.61 ft</b><em>Fr 0.25</em></div><div><span>100-year</span><b style="width:93%">86.15 ft</b><em>Fr 0.22</em></div></div><p>All simulated events remained <strong>subcritical</strong> (Froude number below 1), with water-surface elevation increasing as flood magnitude rose.</p><div class="reach-note"><b>Reach geometry</b><span>Upper reach: 0.20 mi / 1,056 ft</span><span>River-station spacing: 0.10 mi / 528 ft</span></div></div><figure><img src="assets/hec-ras-xyz-plot.png" alt="HEC-RAS XYZ plot showing 10, 50 and 100 year water surface planes"><figcaption>HEC-RAS XYZ plot: water-surface planes expand across the lower reach as the event grows.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="hec-bottom"><article><p class="tag">ROUGHNESS ZONING</p><h3>LOB · channel · ROB</h3><p>Different Manning’s n values model the smoother main channel separately from rougher vegetated or irregular overbanks, improving hydraulic realism.</p></article><article><p class="tag">VELOCITY &amp; RISK · RS 9.6</p><h3>Higher event, higher erosive potential</h3><div class="velocity"><span>10-year</span><b>2.42 ft/s</b><span>100-year</span><b>4.81 ft/s</b></div><p>The near-doubling in velocity indicates a higher likelihood of erosive shear during the 100-year event.</p></article><article><p class="tag">RATING-CURVE INTERPRETATION</p><h3>Why the curve flattens</h3><p>The lower flow is constrained in a narrow trapezoidal channel. Once flow reaches wider overbanks, added discharge spreads laterally, so stage rises less rapidly.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">ENGINEERING DEVELOPMENT</p><h2>A continuous investigation,<br><span>not seven isolated labs.</span></h2><p>This body of work developed my ability to collect experimental data, translate it into governing hydraulic quantities, assess uncertainty, and use professional software to test system-level design decisions.</p><div class="software-row"><span>EPANET</span><span>HEC-RAS</span><span>Excel</span><span>Hydraulic bench testing</span></div></section></main><footer><span>© 2026 Yousif Shasha</span><span>CVL 502 · Hydraulic Engineering</span></footer><script src="script.js"></script></body></html>

