<section class="s-hero"><div><a class="back" href="index.html#projects">← Portfolio</a><p class="eyebrow">PROJECT 01 · CVL 320 + CVL 420 · STRUCTURAL MECHANICS</p><h1>Structural<br><span>Mechanics</span></h1><p class="lead">This portfolio presents six structural mechanics experiments completed in CVL 320 and CVL 420. The projects investigated stress concentration, compression, truss-member forces, beam deflection, flexural stress, and torsion using aluminum, timber, and composite specimens. Each specimen was documented with dimensioned AutoCAD drawings, analyzed through hand calculations, processed in Excel, and evaluated by comparing theoretical results with strain-gauge, dial-gauge, load-cell, and data-acquisition measurements.</p><div class="chips"><span>06 experiments</span><span>Aluminum + timber</span><span>AutoCAD · hand calculations · Excel · testing</span></div></div><div class="hero-cad"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 430" role="img" aria-label="AutoCAD structural laboratory drawing"><g class="gridlines"><path d="M0 50H660M0 100H660M0 150H660M0 200H660M0 250H660M0 300H660M0 350H660M0 400H660M50 0V430M100 0V430M150 0V430M200 0V430M250 0V430M300 0V430M350 0V430M400 0V430M450 0V430M500 0V430M550 0V430M600 0V430"/></g><g class="cad"><path d="M70 315H590M70 315L170 155L270 315L370 155L470 315L590 155M170 155H370M370 155H590"/><circle cx="270" cy="315" r="6"/><path d="M270 325V392M250 365L270 392L290 365"/><text x="282" y="382">P</text><path d="M70 335V370M590 335V370M70 357H590"/><text x="290" y="353">1040 mm</text></g></svg><small>MODEL SPACE / STRUCTURAL LAB SERIES / NTS</small></div></section>
<section class="workflow"><p class="eyebrow">PROJECT WORKFLOW</p><div><article><b>01</b><h3>AutoCAD definition</h3><p>Plan, elevation, section, dimensions, materials, supports, loads, and gauge locations.</p></article><article><b>02</b><h3>Hand model</h3><p>Free-body diagram, governing equation, substituted values, units, and engineering check.</p></article><article><b>03</b><h3>Excel model</h3><p>Raw measurements, formula-driven tables, comparisons, percent differences, and plots.</p></article><article><b>04</b><h3>Laboratory evidence</h3><p>Strain gauges, dial gauges, load cells, and data acquisition validate the idealized model.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="part-index"><a href="#part-1"><b>01</b><span>Stress concentration</span></a><a href="#part-2"><b>02</b><span>Hollow cylinder</span></a><a href="#part-3"><b>03</b><span>Aluminum truss</span></a><a href="#part-4"><b>04</b><span>Beam deflection</span></a><a href="#part-5"><b>05</b><span>Flexural stress</span></a><a href="#part-6"><b>06</b><span>Torsion</span></a></section>

<section id="part-1" class="lab light"><header><p>PART 01 · CVL 320</p><h2>Stress concentration<br>in a stepped aluminum plate</h2><span>Axial tension · 10 gauges · E = 70,000 MPa</span></header><div class="lab-grid"><figure class="cad-card"><svg viewBox="0 0 700 390" role="img" aria-label="Dimensioned stepped plate drawing"><defs><marker id="a1" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="3.5" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M7 0L0 3.5L7 7"/></marker></defs><g class="shape"><path d="M265 40H435V125Q435 150 405 165V330H295V165Q265 150 265 125Z"/><path class="center" d="M350 20V355"/><path class="gauge" d="M320 190V225M350 190V225M380 190V225M320 70V105M350 70V105M380 70V105"/><path d="M350 330V375" marker-end="url(#a1)"/></g><g class="dim"><path d="M265 25H435" marker-start="url(#a1)" marker-end="url(#a1)"/><text x="315" y="18">D = 71 mm</text><path d="M295 345H405" marker-start="url(#a1)" marker-end="url(#a1)"/><text x="205" y="350">d = 40 mm</text><text x="450" y="95">Aluminum plate</text><text x="450" y="118">Gauge lines 7-8-9</text><text x="450" y="205">Gauge line 4-5-6</text><text x="370" y="382">TENSION</text></g></svg><figcaption>AUTOCAD · FRONT ELEVATION</figcaption></figure><div class="calc-stack"><article class="hand"><label>HAND CHECK</label><code>σ<sub>avg</sub> = F / A<br>K<sub>t,theory</sub> = 1.50<br>At F = 490 N:<br>σ<sub>avg</sub> = 5.75 MPa<br>σ<sub>max,theory</sub> = 1.50(5.75)<br><b>= 8.63 MPa</b></code></article><article class="sheet"><label>EXCEL RESULT SNAPSHOT</label><table><tr><th>Load</th><th>Avg. stress (MPa)</th><th>Max stress (MPa)</th><th>K<sub>exp</sub></th></tr><tr><td>196 N</td><td>2.30</td><td>1.54</td><td>0.67</td></tr><tr><td>392 N</td><td>4.60</td><td>3.50</td><td>0.76</td></tr><tr><td>490 N</td><td>5.75</td><td>4.62</td><td>0.80</td></tr></table><small>Formula cells compare measured maximum with F/A.</small></article></div></div><div class="finding"><b>Reading the result</b><p>Stress rose approximately linearly with load, but the measured peaks remained below the theoretical notch prediction. Gauge line 4-5-6 was the more critical measured section.</p></div></section>

<section id="part-2" class="lab dark"><header><p>PART 02 · CVL 320</p><h2>Compression of a<br>thin-walled cylinder</h2><span>Aluminum tube · eight gauges · hydraulic loading</span></header><div class="lab-grid"><figure class="cad-card"><svg viewBox="0 0 700 390"><defs><marker id="a2" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="3.5" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M7 0L0 3.5L7 7"/></marker></defs><g class="shape"><rect x="110" y="55" width="180" height="265"/><path class="center" d="M200 30V345"/><circle cx="485" cy="185" r="95"/><circle cx="485" cy="185" r="89"/><path class="gauge" d="M155 105V155M200 105V155M245 105V155"/><path d="M200 10V52" marker-end="url(#a2)"/></g><g class="dim"><path d="M75 55V320" marker-start="url(#a2)" marker-end="url(#a2)"/><text x="25" y="195" transform="rotate(-90 25 195)">405 mm</text><path d="M390 300H580" marker-start="url(#a2)" marker-end="url(#a2)"/><text x="435" y="325">Ø100 mm OD</text><path d="M485 185L574 185" marker-end="url(#a2)"/><text x="455" y="165">t = 3.0 mm</text><text x="80" y="22">COMPRESSION</text><text x="410" y="55">SECTION A-A</text></g></svg><figcaption>AUTOCAD · ELEVATION + SECTION · 100 OD × 3 wall × 405 long</figcaption></figure><div class="calc-stack"><article class="hand"><label>HAND CHECK · 300 kg</label><code>D<sub>i</sub> = 100 - 2(3) = 94 mm<br>A = π/4(D<sub>o</sub>² - D<sub>i</sub>²)<br><b>A = 914.20 mm²</b><br>P = 300(9.81) = 2,943 N<br>σ<sub>theory</sub> = P/A<br><b>= 3.219 MPa compression</b></code></article><article class="sheet"><label>EXCEL COMPARISON</label><table><tr><th>Load</th><th>σ<sub>theory</sub> (MPa)</th><th>σ<sub>measured avg.</sub> (MPa)</th><th>|Δ|</th></tr><tr><td>300 kg</td><td>3.22</td><td>4.73</td><td>46.9%</td></tr><tr><td>600 kg</td><td>6.44</td><td>8.63</td><td>34.0%</td></tr><tr><td>900 kg</td><td>9.66</td><td>12.40</td><td>28.4%</td></tr><tr><td>1200 kg</td><td>12.88</td><td>17.07</td><td>32.6%</td></tr></table></article></div></div><div class="finding"><b>Reading the result</b><p>Gauge 7 consistently recorded the largest compression. The nonuniform field points to eccentric loading, imperfect end contact, wall variation, and local bending that the uniform P/A model cannot capture.</p></div></section>

<section id="part-3" class="lab light"><header><p>PART 03 · CVL 320</p><h2>Force paths in an<br>aluminum model truss</h2><span>Axial member force · tension/compression · method of joints</span></header><div class="lab-grid"><figure class="cad-card wide-cad"><svg viewBox="0 0 800 390"><defs><marker id="a3" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="3.5" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M7 0L0 3.5L7 7"/></marker></defs><g class="shape truss"><path d="M70 300H730M70 300L200 105L330 300L460 105L590 300L730 105M200 105H460H730M200 105V300M460 105V300M590 300V105"/><circle cx="330" cy="300" r="7"/><path d="M330 310V370" marker-end="url(#a3)"/></g><g class="dim"><path d="M70 335H200" marker-start="url(#a3)" marker-end="url(#a3)"/><path d="M200 335H330" marker-start="url(#a3)" marker-end="url(#a3)"/><text x="110" y="358">260</text><text x="240" y="358">260</text><path d="M760 105V300" marker-start="url(#a3)" marker-end="url(#a3)"/><text x="770" y="210" transform="rotate(90 770 210)">157 mm</text><text x="120" y="185" transform="rotate(-56 120 185)">304 mm diagonal</text><text x="345" y="385">LOAD</text><text x="510" y="45">Aluminum flat members · t = 2.13 mm</text><text x="125" y="287">12</text><text x="255" y="287">2</text><text x="315" y="94">8</text><text x="125" y="210">4</text><text x="255" y="210">6</text><text x="184" y="210">10</text><text x="445" y="210">7</text></g></svg><figcaption>AUTOCAD · TRUSS ELEVATION</figcaption></figure><div class="calc-stack"><article class="hand"><label>HAND CHECK · MEMBER 2</label><code>A = b t = 12.5(2.13)<br><b>A = 26.625 mm²</b><br>At 908 g: ε = 6 με<br>F<sub>exp</sub> = EεA<br>= 70,000(6×10⁻⁶)(26.625)<br><b>= 11.18 N tension</b></code></article><article class="sheet"><label>EXCEL MEMBER SUMMARY</label><table><tr><th>Member</th><th>Sign</th><th>E avg. (MPa)</th></tr><tr><td>2 / 12</td><td>Tension</td><td>54,810 / 58,016</td></tr><tr><td>4 / 6</td><td>Compression</td><td>41,063 / 95,775</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Zero-force</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>8 / 10</td><td>Mixed</td><td>371,998 / 199,264</td></tr></table><small>Large scatter identifies the limits of very small strain readings and assumed member width.</small></article></div></div><div class="finding"><b>Reading the result</b><p>The signs successfully map the truss load path: positive strain marks tension and negative strain compression. Near-zero readings are especially sensitive to gauge resolution, joint friction, and construction tolerances.</p></div></section>

<section id="part-4" class="lab dark"><header><p>PART 04 · CVL 420</p><h2>Deflection of timber<br>and composite beams</h2><span>Four-point bending · transformed section · dial gauge</span></header><div class="lab-grid"><figure class="cad-card"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 430"><defs><marker id="a4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="3.5" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M7 0L0 3.5L7 7"/></marker></defs><g class="shape"><rect x="65" y="55" width="630" height="38"/><circle cx="65" cy="112" r="16"/><circle cx="695" cy="112" r="16"/><path d="M275 10V52M485 10V52" marker-end="url(#a4)"/><rect x="105" y="225" width="85" height="150"/><rect x="105" y="218" width="85" height="7" class="metal"/><rect x="430" y="240" width="85" height="140"/><path d="M430 275H515M430 310H515M430 345H515"/></g><g class="dim"><path d="M65 150H695" marker-start="url(#a4)" marker-end="url(#a4)"/><text x="350" y="172">900 mm</text><text x="248" y="30">P/2</text><text x="458" y="30">P/2</text><text x="97" y="205">A · composite section</text><text x="101" y="402">35 × 88 timber + 2.3 Al</text><text x="410" y="225">B · glued timber</text><text x="425" y="402">35 × 70 (4 × 17 mm)</text></g></svg><figcaption>AUTOCAD · BEAM ELEVATION + TWO TEST SECTIONS</figcaption></figure><div class="calc-stack"><article class="hand"><label>HAND DEFLECTION MODEL</label><code>v = Pa(3L² - 4a²) / 24EI<br>L = 900 mm · a = 300 mm<br>Composite I = 3.083×10⁶ mm⁴<br>Glued I = 0.986×10⁶ mm⁴<br>At 80 kg:<br><b>v<sub>theory</sub> = 0.732 / 2.288 mm</b></code></article><article class="sheet"><label>EXCEL LOAD-DEFLECTION</label><table><tr><th>Load</th><th>Composite deflection</th><th>Glued deflection</th></tr><tr><td>0 kg</td><td>-0.019</td><td>-0.016</td></tr><tr><td>20 kg</td><td>0.111</td><td>0.215</td></tr><tr><td>40 kg</td><td>0.239</td><td>0.415</td></tr><tr><td>80 kg</td><td>0.507</td><td>0.825</td></tr></table><small>Measured central deflection, mm. The reinforced section remained stiffer across the loading range.</small></article></div></div><div class="finding"><b>Reading the result</b><p>The aluminum-reinforced timber beam deflected 38.5% less than the four-piece glued beam at 80 kg. The nearly linear trends support elastic beam theory; unloading offsets reveal small zeroing, seating, or hysteresis effects.</p></div></section>

<section id="part-5" class="lab light"><header><p>PART 05 · CVL 420</p><h2>Flexural stress across<br>an aluminum I-beam</h2><span>Four-point bending · strain profile · neutral axis</span></header><div class="lab-grid"><figure class="cad-card"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 430"><defs><marker id="a5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="3.5" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M7 0L0 3.5L7 7"/></marker></defs><g class="shape"><rect x="50" y="55" width="660" height="34"/><circle cx="50" cy="108" r="15"/><circle cx="710" cy="108" r="15"/><path d="M270 10V53M490 10V53" marker-end="url(#a5)"/><path d="M310 220H450V238H389V382H450V400H310V382H371V238H310Z"/><path class="gauge" d="M330 226H430M330 278H430M330 338H430M330 392H430"/></g><g class="dim"><path d="M50 145H710" marker-start="url(#a5)" marker-end="url(#a5)"/><text x="340" y="168">1350 mm = 3 × 450</text><path d="M485 220V400" marker-start="url(#a5)" marker-end="url(#a5)"/><text x="505" y="315">152 mm deep</text><path d="M310 205H450" marker-start="url(#a5)" marker-end="url(#a5)"/><text x="345" y="195">76 mm</text><text x="205" y="250">t<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">f</tspan> = 8.02 mm</text><text x="205" y="320">t<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">w</tspan> = 6.57 mm</text><text x="335" y="420">GAUGES 1–4</text></g></svg><figcaption>AUTOCAD · FOUR-POINT BENDING + I-SECTION GAUGE LEVELS</figcaption></figure><div class="calc-stack"><article class="hand"><label>HAND CHECK · 200 kg</label><code>R<sub>A</sub> = P/2 = 981 N<br>M = 981(450)<br><b>= 441,450 N·mm</b><br>I<sub>x</sub> = 7.700×10⁶ mm⁴<br>σ<sub>top</sub> = -My/I, y = 76 mm<br><b>= -4.357 MPa</b></code></article><article class="sheet"><label>EXCEL / STRAIN RESULT</label><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td>Experimental neutral axis</td><td>76.87 mm</td></tr><tr><td>Theoretical neutral axis</td><td>76.00 mm</td></tr><tr><td>Difference</td><td>1.14%</td></tr><tr><td>Top stress at 800 kg</td><td>-11.50 MPa</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom stress at 800 kg</td><td>+12.78 MPa</td></tr></table></article></div></div><div class="finding"><b>Reading the result</b><p>The measured neutral axis landed within 0.87 mm of the theoretical centroid. Compression at the top and tension at the bottom form the expected near-linear flexural stress profile.</p></div></section>

<section id="part-6" class="lab dark"><header><p>PART 06 · CVL 420</p><h2>Torsional behaviour<br>of an aluminum beam</h2><span>Cantilever tube · strain rosettes · angle of twist</span></header><div class="lab-grid"><figure class="cad-card"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 430"><defs><marker id="a6" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="3.5" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M7 0L0 3.5L7 7"/></marker></defs><g class="shape"><rect x="75" y="145" width="520" height="90" rx="45"/><path d="M75 105V275M60 115L75 105M60 145L75 135M60 175L75 165M60 205L75 195M60 235L75 225M60 265L75 255"/><path d="M595 115V290M595 290H700M650 290V370"/><circle cx="595" cy="190" r="42"/><path class="gauge" d="M250 150L290 190M250 230L290 190M330 150L370 190M330 230L370 190"/><path d="M650 365V310" marker-end="url(#a6)"/><path d="M620 105A60 60 0 0 1 690 160" marker-end="url(#a6)"/></g><g class="dim"><text x="90" y="125">FIXED END</text><text x="230" y="265">ALUMINUM CIRCULAR BEAM</text><text x="225" y="300">strain gauges 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9</text><text x="625" y="395">P</text><text x="675" y="95">T = P(430 mm)</text><text x="485" y="350">lever arm = 430 mm</text></g></svg><figcaption>AUTOCAD · CANTILEVER TORSION SETUP · lever arm inferred from reported T/P</figcaption></figure><div class="calc-stack"><article class="hand"><label>HAND TORSION MODEL</label><code>T = Pr = 196.2(430)<br><b>= 84,366 N·mm</b><br>τ = Tc/J<br>θ = TL/GJ<br>At T = 84.37 kN·mm:<br><b>τ<sub>theory</sub> = 2.021 MPa</b><br><b>θ = 0.001227 rad</b></code></article><article class="sheet"><label>EXCEL TORQUE RESPONSE</label><table><tr><th>Load</th><th>Torque</th><th>τ theory</th><th>θ</th></tr><tr><td>196.2 N</td><td>84.37 kN·mm</td><td>2.021</td><td>0.001227</td></tr><tr><td>392.4 N</td><td>168.73 kN·mm</td><td>4.042</td><td>0.002453</td></tr><tr><td>784.8 N</td><td>337.46 kN·mm</td><td>8.084</td><td>0.004906</td></tr></table><small>Stress in MPa; twist in radians. Both scale linearly with applied torque.</small></article></div></div><div class="finding"><b>Reading the result</b><p>The theoretical model predicts proportional torque, shear stress, and twist. Unequal gauge stresses reflect orientation, local strain transfer, zero offsets, and the difficulty of translating a multi-gauge state into ideal pure torsion.</p></div></section>
<section class="synthesis"><p class="eyebrow">ENGINEERING DEVELOPMENT</p><h2>The drawing is the first calculation.</h2><p>Across all six experiments, the most important habit was maintaining a traceable chain from geometry and material, to idealized mechanics, to spreadsheet formulas, to measured behaviour. The discrepancies are not failures; they show exactly where alignment, contact, material variability, gauge placement, and model assumptions influence structural response.</p><div><span>AutoCAD-style drafting</span><span>Excel / Google Sheets</span><span>Strain-gauge analysis</span><span>Beam theory</span><span>Truss statics</span><span>Torsion</span></div></section></main><footer><span>© 2026 Yousif Shasha</span><span>Project 04 · Structural Behaviour Laboratory</span></footer><script src="script.js"></script></body></html>

