    <!-- =========================================
         LEVELING — PART ONE
         ========================================= -->
    <section class="section light-alt" id="leveling">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 · LEVELING — PART ONE</p>
        <h2>Determining elevation differences with an automatic level.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="leveling-layout">
        <div class="process-list">
          <div class="process-step"><span>01</span><div><h3>Instrument setup</h3><p>The automatic level was mounted securely on the tripod and leveled so that the line of sight was horizontal.</p></div></div>
          <div class="process-step"><span>02</span><div><h3>Backsight reading</h3><p>The leveling staff was placed on a point with a known elevation, or benchmark. The staff reading was recorded as the <strong>backsight</strong> and used to establish the instrument height.</p></div></div>
          <div class="process-step"><span>03</span><div><h3>Foresight reading</h3><p>The staff was moved to the next point, or turning point. The reading taken from the same horizontal line of sight was recorded as the <strong>foresight</strong>.</p></div></div>
          <div class="process-step"><span>04</span><div><h3>Elevation calculation</h3><p>The difference in elevation between the two points was determined from the backsight and foresight readings.</p></div></div>
          <div class="formula large">ΔH = Backsight − Foresight</div>
        </div>

        <div class="leveling-side">
          <div class="process-diagram">
            <div class="level-point lp1"><b>Benchmark</b><span>Known elevation</span></div>
            <div class="sight-line"></div>
            <div class="level-instrument"><span>Automatic<br>Level</span></div>
            <div class="level-point lp2"><b>Turning Point</b><span>Foresight</span></div>
            <div class="reading-card rc1"><strong>Backsight</strong><small>First staff reading</small></div>
            <div class="reading-card rc2"><strong>Foresight</strong><small>Next staff reading</small></div>
          </div>
          <div class="method-note"><strong>Why the setup matters:</strong> Keeping the instrument properly leveled and using a consistent line of sight improves the reliability of the elevation difference.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- =========================================
         DIFFERENTIAL LEVELING
         ========================================= -->
    <section class="section" id="differential-leveling">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 · DIFFERENTIAL LEVELING</p>
        <h2>Checking closure and adjusting elevations.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="two-column">
        <div>
          <p>
            A closed leveling loop was used to compare the starting and ending benchmark
            elevations. The measurements were processed into elevations, checked for misclosure,
            compared with the permissible closure, and adjusted where required.
          </p>
          <p>
            This part of the work demonstrated that field measurements need a quality check before
            they are used as final engineering values.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="result-card">
          <span>RESULT CHECK</span>
          <div class="result-row"><b>Misclosure</b><strong>0.01 ft</strong></div>
          <div class="result-row"><b>Permissible closure</b><strong>0.0197 ft</strong></div>
          <div class="result-row"><b>Outcome</b><strong class="accepted">Acceptable ✓</strong></div>
          <p>Elevations were adjusted using proportional correction after the closure check.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

